{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XRhMXTTSwK9Piti7B1EiYX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Yc7ePqudoBABK4RPyPa9hg","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"claude","description":"Reviewer 1: methods + experiments","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 in a full-mode academic paper review. Your focus: **methods + experiments**. Do NOT evaluate novelty/related work or formatting.\n\nThe paper: \"Why Structure-Grounded Hard Negatives Don't Help Code Retrieval (Yet): An Empirical Analysis of Three Negative Mining Strategies\"\n\n## Paper Summary\nThis paper compares three negative mining strategies for contrastive code context retrieval: random, embedding-distance, and structure-grounded (dataflow-aware via CodeQL). The structure-grounded approach uses inter-procedural dataflow connectivity to select lexically similar but structurally disconnected files as hard negatives. The main finding is negative: both hard negative strategies underperform random negatives across all settings.\n\nKey claims:\n- Both hard negative strategies underperform random: retrieval (−3.1pp and −3.5pp R@5), SWE-bench Lite (−0.6pp and −1.0pp), RepoBench-R (−2.1pp and −2.9pp)\n- Root causes: (1) only 14% of training instances have non-trivial structural signal, (2) CodeQL 2-hop has irreconcilable tradeoff between density and feasibility, (3) call-graph captures only one dependency type\n- Manual analysis of 50 failures: dataflow helps 8%, harms 36%\n\n## Your Review Dimensions\n\n### 1. Method Description\n- Is the formalization in Equation 1 complete? Does it match what's actually implemented?\n- Are key hyperparameters and training details (LoRA config, learning rate, batch size, etc.) fully specified?\n- Is the CodeQL extraction pipeline described with enough detail to reproduce?\n- The threshold τ in the dataflow-aware strategy — how is it set? Is sensitivity analysis provided?\n\n### 2. Experimental Design\n- 3 strategies × 3 seeds × 4 eval settings — is this sufficient?\n- Pool size = 200 for retrieval eval: is this realistic? How does it compare to real-world retrieval scenarios?\n- SWE-bench Lite: 300 instances with Qwen3.6-27B agent — is the agent confound adequately controlled?\n- RepoBench-R: 1200 samples — is cross-repo transfer the right evaluation for a training-time intervention?\n- Baselines: is SweRank a fair comparison given it's trained on a larger corpus?\n- Missing baselines: no comparison with other structural approaches (ARISE, LocAgent, RANGER)?\n\n### 3. Data Presentation\n- Tables 1-8: do they support the claims made?\n- Statistical significance: are error bars / p-values reported consistently?\n- Table 1: Z% is the key metric — is the methodology for computing it sound?\n- Table 2: ±std across 3 seeds — is 3 seeds enough for reliable variance estimates?\n- Table 3: SWE-bench results are within ±2.5pp CI — paper acknowledges this but still draws conclusions\n- Table 4: hop-bounding tradeoff — R@5 values use which strategy?\n- Table 5: scaling ablation — only for dataflow strategy, not embedding-distance?\n- Table 6: stratified analysis — interesting but post-hoc?\n- Table 7: fusion head — is this a fair test? What fusion architecture was used?\n- Table 8: 50 manual cases — sample size concerns? Inter-annotator agreement?\n\n### 4. Specific Issues to Flag\n- The paper says \"Z%\" but Table 1 header uses \"Z%\" while §2 contribution 2 says \"(Z%)\" — is Z ever defined as a specific number or left as a placeholder?\n- \"Qwen3.6-27B\" — is this a real model? Qwen model naming doesn't follow this convention.\n- SweRank reference has \"arXiv:2405.xxxxx\" — placeholder?\n- The claim \"all hard negatives underperform random when the candidate pool is sufficiently diverse\" is stated as a general principle but only tested in one domain.\n\nThis is a **worst** tier dream paper. Assess whether the quality level is consistent with a \"worst\" tier.\n\nProvide your review in structured format with: (a) Summary of strengths, (b) Summary of weaknesses, (c) Detailed issues list with severity (critical/major/minor), (d) Questions for authors, (e) Overall assessment."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2990,"cache_read_input_tokens":42374,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2990},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66f3a5d4-4ad0-44b0-83d9-d7830f4116d0","uuid":"35631cd8-b99c-4232-95a6-fcb4f1c460c1"}
