{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JSaUiTv4nKs4rab68DQzoN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UYxpM9Jg56xMAdeazbcGzm","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"claude","description":"R1 methods and experiments review","model":"sonnet","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 in an academic paper review. Your SOLE focus: **methods + experiments**. Do NOT evaluate novelty/related work or formatting. Output your review directly — no preamble.\n\nThe paper: \"Why Structure-Grounded Hard Negatives Don't Help Code Retrieval (Yet)\"\n\n## Full Paper Content (8 pages)\n\n**Abstract**: Hard negative mining comparison for contrastive code retrieval. Three strategies: random, embedding-distance, structure-grounded (dataflow-aware via CodeQL). Main finding: both hard negative strategies underperform random: retrieval (−3.1pp and −3.5pp R@5), SWE-bench Lite (−0.6pp and −1.0pp), RepoBench-R (−2.1pp and −2.9pp). Root causes: (1) only 14% have non-trivial structural signal (Z%), (2) hop-bounding tradeoff, (3) call-graph captures only one dependency type. Manual analysis of 50 failures: dataflow helps 8%, harms 36%.\n\n**§3 Method**:\n- §3.1: CodeQL extracts inter-procedural dataflow from 5000 SWE-smith Python repos. Call graph, k-hop reachability between file pairs. Connected set C_q = files reachable within k hops. Non-trivial structural signal when C_q \\ G_q ≠ ∅ (G_q = gold files). Z% = fraction where this holds.\n- §3.2 Three strategies: Random (uniform from D_q\\G_q), Embedding-distance (highest-sim in embedding space, re-mined every epoch), Dataflow-aware (among files with s(x,q)>τ, prefer ¬reachable_G(x,q); falls back to random when C_q = G_q).\n- §3.3: Qwen2.5-Coder-3B with LoRA, ~15K instances, InfoNCE loss, 3 seeds (42,123,456). Eval: R@1/5/10 (pool=200), SWE-bench Lite (300 instances, Qwen3.6-27B agent), RepoBench-R (1200 samples).\n\n**§4 Results**:\n- Table 1: CodeQL stats. 3850/5000 repos succeeded. Z=14% overall. By size: Small(<50 files) 2310 repos, 10920 instances, Z=9.2%; Medium(50-200) 963 repos, 4550 instances, Z=24.8%; Large(>200) 577 repos, 2730 instances, Z=5.1%, timeout=22.1%.\n- Table 2: Retrieval (pool=200, 3 seeds). Random: R@1=0.415±0.009, R@5=0.783±0.008, R@10=0.871±0.007. Emb-dist: 0.378±0.007, 0.748±0.007, 0.842±0.007 (Δ=-0.035). Dataflow: 0.382±0.008, 0.752±0.007, 0.845±0.007 (Δ=-0.031).\n- Table 3: SWE-bench Lite: Random 24.3% resolve, 0.68 File R@5; Emb-dist 23.3%, 0.66; Dataflow 23.7%, 0.67; SweRank 24.7%, 0.71. RepoBench-R: Random MRR=0.534, R@5=0.712; Emb-dist 0.498, 0.683; Dataflow 0.507, 0.691.\n- Table 4: Hop tradeoff. 1-hop: Z=6.3%, timeout=4.8%, gold_cov=0.51, mean|C_q|=3.2, R@5=0.771. 2-hop: Z=14%, timeout=14.2%, gold_cov=0.72, |C_q|=18.7, R@5=0.752. 3-hop: Z=22.5%, timeout=38.6%, gold_cov=0.84, |C_q|=67.3, R@5=0.738.\n- Table 5: Scaling ablation (dataflow only). 1K: random 0.742, DF 0.721 (Δ=-0.021); 3K: 0.768 vs 0.743 (Δ=-0.025); 5K: 0.783 vs 0.752 (Δ=-0.031).\n- Table 6: Emb-dist harm stratified. High intra-sim (N=410): Δ=-0.067, p=0.008. Low intra-sim (N=390): Δ=-0.008, p=0.68.\n- Table 7: Fusion head. Emb-only R@5=0.779; Structural-only 0.612; Combined 0.752 (Δ=-0.027 vs emb-only).\n- Table 8: 50 manual failures. Cross-module 12 (4 helpful, 8 no); Single-file 18 neutral; Test-related 8 neutral; Config/setup 7 (0/5 harmful); Doc-adjacent 5 (0/2 harmful). Total: 8% helpful, 36% harmful, 56% neutral.\n\n## Review Instructions\n\nAssess:\n1. **Method completeness**: Is the formalization sound? Key missing details (τ threshold, LoRA config, LR, batch size, epochs, embedding model for emb-distance strategy)?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: 3 seeds sufficient? Pool=200 realistic? SWE-bench agent confound? RepoBench-R as transfer eval?\n3. **Data presentation**: Do tables support claims? Statistical significance reported consistently? Table 2 uses ±std from 3 seeds — reliable? Table 3 SWE-bench within CI but paper draws conclusions anyway?\n4. **Red flags**: \"Qwen3.6-27B\" — not a real model name. SweRank ref has placeholder \"arXiv:2405.xxxxx\". Table 1 total: 2310+963+577=3850 repos but instances 10920+4550+2730=18200 — these are from 3850, not 5000. Contribution 2 says \"(Z%)\" with literal \"Z%\" — possible placeholder.\n\nProvide: (a) Strengths, (b) Weaknesses, (c) Detailed issues with severity [critical/major/minor], (d) Questions for authors, (e) Assessment score."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2461,"cache_read_input_tokens":45364,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2461},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66f3a5d4-4ad0-44b0-83d9-d7830f4116d0","uuid":"e8f1704a-490c-4e8e-8115-438f13bead10"}
