{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JSaUiTv4nKs4rab68DQzoN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GpZzpnDniSfT4Gngd458nq","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"claude","description":"R2 novelty and related work review","model":"sonnet","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 in an academic paper review. Your SOLE focus: **novelty + related work + story consistency**. Do NOT evaluate method implementation or experimental design. Output your review directly — no preamble.\n\nThe paper: \"Why Structure-Grounded Hard Negatives Don't Help Code Retrieval (Yet)\"\n\n## Full Paper Content (8 pages)\n\n**Abstract**: Compares random, embedding-distance, structure-grounded (dataflow via CodeQL) negative mining for code retrieval. Both hard negatives underperform random: retrieval (−3.1pp, −3.5pp R@5), SWE-bench Lite (−0.6pp, −1.0pp), RepoBench-R (−2.1pp, −2.9pp). Root causes: Z=14% structural signal, hop-bounding tradeoff, call-graph limitation. Failure taxonomy: 8% helpful, 36% harmful.\n\n**§1 Intro**: Frames structure-grounded negatives as \"intuitively appealing\" — lexically similar but structurally disconnected. Formal definition Eq.1: x⁻ = argmax_{x∈D: s(x,q)>τ ∧ ¬reachable_G(x,q)} s(x,q). Three contributions claimed: (1) systematic comparison at scale, (2) why CodeQL 2-hop fails, (3) failure taxonomy.\n\n**§2 Related Work** (3 short paragraphs):\n- Code Retrieval for Repair: SweRank (dense), LocAgent/RANGER/CodexGraph (graph at inference), ARISE (intra-procedural)\n- Hard Negative Mining: Robinson et al. 2021 (only citation for HN literature). CoCoHaNeRe mentioned for code search hard negatives.\n- Structural Analysis: CodeQL for inter-procedural analysis.\n\n**§7 Discussion**: Random wins because large pools implicitly sample structural diversity. Results don't rule out structure-grounded training in general — just 2-hop call-graph. Future: import graphs, type hierarchies, test-source mappings, learned structural embeddings. Cross-domain applicability note. Practical recommendation: random negatives with large pools.\n\n**§8 Conclusion**: Repeats main findings. \"We hope this analysis saves future researchers from rediscovering these limitations.\"\n\n**References** (only 9):\n1. Jiménez et al. 2024 - SWE-bench (ICLR)\n2. Robinson et al. 2021 - Hard negatives (ICLR)\n3. Zhang et al. 2024 - SweRank (arXiv **2405.xxxxx** — PLACEHOLDER)\n4. Liu et al. 2024 - RepoBench (ICLR)\n5. Chen et al. 2024 - Hard negatives in code search (ACL)\n6. GitHub 2024 - CodeQL docs\n7. Pei et al. 2024 - ARISE (ICSE)\n8. SWE-smith Contributors 2025\n9. Hu et al. 2022 - LoRA (ICLR)\n\n## Review Dimensions\n\n### 1. Novelty\n- Is \"negative result\" framing enough? Three claimed contributions — independently valuable?\n- Does it go beyond \"we tried X, it failed\"?\n- The Z% threshold concept, conditional embedding-distance finding (§5.3) — novel?\n- Compare to existing negative result papers in ML.\n\n### 2. Related Work Gaps\n- Only 9 references total — extremely thin for empirical analysis\n- SweRank has PLACEHOLDER arXiv ID\n- CoCoHaNeRe mentioned but NOT in references\n- LocAgent, RANGER, CodexGraph mentioned but NOT cited\n- Missing: GraphCodeBERT, CodeBERT, UniXcoder, other code encoders\n- Missing: curriculum learning, progressive hard negatives\n- Missing: AST-based, PDG, control flow graph approaches\n- Missing: Kalantidis et al. 2020 (hard negatives in vision), Xiong et al. 2021 (ANCE)\n- No coverage of in-batch negatives vs. mined negatives distinction\n\n### 3. Story Consistency\n- Abstract says \"across all evaluation settings\" but SWE-bench results are within CI (±2.5pp) — overclaimed\n- Contribution 1 says \"sufficiently diverse\" — qualifier not empirically established\n- §5.2 claims more data won't help — but only tests to 5K, not 15K full scale\n- Title says \"yet\" implying future potential, but §7 discussion is brief\n- Eq.1 formalization obscures that method degenerates to random 86% of time\n- \"Qwen3.6-27B\" in §3.3 — not a real model designation (Qwen uses Qwen2.5-XB naming)\n- §2 contribution 2 literally says \"(Z%)\" — looks like an unfilled placeholder\n\nProvide: (a) Strengths, (b) Weaknesses, (c) Detailed issues with severity [critical/major/minor], (d) Questions for authors, (e) Assessment score."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2461,"cache_read_input_tokens":45364,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2461},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"66f3a5d4-4ad0-44b0-83d9-d7830f4116d0","uuid":"c9917f8b-fba1-475d-bd28-54d0c59f8d71"}
