 pool size threshold makes a pool \"sufficiently diverse\"?"," ","-9. **Scaling claim overreach.** §5.2 claims \"This rules out the hypothesis that more data would rescue the approach\" based on scaling only to 5K instances, when the full training set is 15K. Testing 1/3 of the data range does not definitively rule out the hypothesis.","+9. **Scaling claim overreach.** Section 5.2 claims \"This rules out the hypothesis that more data would rescue the approach\" based on scaling only to 5K instances, when the full training set is 15K. Testing 1/3 of the data range does not definitively rule out the hypothesis."," "," #### Minor"," "]},{"oldStart":119,"oldLines":16,"newStart":126,"newLines":28,"lines":[" "," | Check | Verdict |"," |-------|---------|","-| Abstract claims ↔ Results | **Inconsistent** — SWE-bench \"across all settings\" claim contradicts §4.3 CI analysis |","-| Eq. 1 formalization ↔ Actual method | **Misleading** — 86% fallback to random not flagged in formal presentation |","-| Contributions claimed ↔ Delivered | **Partial** — Contribution 1 supported; Contribution 2 has placeholder; Contribution 3 undersized |","-| Title \"Yet\" ↔ Evidence | **Unsupported** — no pilot data for alternative structural signals |","-| Pilot Z=40.8% ↔ Full Z=14% | **Consistent** — adequately explained by sampling bias |","+| Abstract claims vs. Results | **Inconsistent** — SWE-bench \"across all settings\" claim contradicts Section 4.3 CI analysis |","+| Eq. 1 formalization vs. Actual method | **Misleading** — 86% fallback to random not flagged in formal presentation |","+| Contributions claimed vs. Delivered | **Partial** — Contribution 1 supported; Contribution 2 has placeholder; Contribution 3 undersized |","+| Title \"Yet\" vs. Evidence | **Unsupported** — no pilot data for alternative structural signals |","+| Pilot Z=40.8% vs. Full Z=14% | **Consistent** — adequately explained by sampling bias |"," "," ---"," "," ## Cross-Reviewer Synthesis (Roundtable Steps 2-3)"," ","+### R1 commentary on R2's findings","+","+- The missing references (CoCoHaNeRe, LocAgent, etc.) are a methodological problem too — without proper baselines cited, it's impossible to assess whether the experimental comparison is complete.","+- The Eq. 1 formalization vs. 86% fallback issue raised by R2 has direct experimental implications: the \"dataflow-aware\" strategy is effectively \"86% random + 14% dataflow-modified.\" This should have been ablated as a mixture rather than treated as a distinct strategy.","+- The fabricated model name \"Qwen3.6-27B\" makes the entire SWE-bench evaluation unverifiable from both perspectives.","+","+### R2 commentary on R1's findings","+","+- The missing hyperparameters (tau, LoRA config, etc.) compound the citation problem — not only is the method poorly contextualized in the literature, it's also unreproducible.","+- The pool size = 200 concern connects to the \"sufficiently diverse\" claim — if the pool is small, the entire argument about random negatives implicitly sampling diversity is weakened.","+- The Table 1 to Section 3.3 instance count discrepancy (18.2K vs. ~15K) is a data integrity issue that further weakens confidence.","+"," ### Convergence"," "," Both reviewers independently converge on **reject** with overlapping critical concerns:"]},{"oldStart":137,"oldLines":7,"newStart":156,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," 2. **Overclaimed abstract** — The \"across all evaluation settings\" claim is directly contradicted by the paper's own statistical analysis of SWE-bench results."," ","-3. **Unreproducible** — Missing τ threshold, LoRA config, learning rate, batch size, epochs, embedding model, fusion architecture, InfoNCE temperature.","+3. **Unreproducible** — Missing tau threshold, LoRA config, learning rate, batch size, epochs, embedding model, fusion architecture, InfoNCE temperature."," "," 4. **Inadequate scholarship** — 9 references for an empirical analysis paper; the hard negative mining literature is represented by a single citation."," "]},{"oldStart":146,"oldLines":7,"newStart":165,"newLines":7,"lines":[" - R1 (methods/experiments) finds the experimental *structure* reasonable and gives credit for the hop-bounding analysis and stratified embedding findings. Leans **weak reject**."," - R2 (novelty/related work) views the manuscript as fundamentally unready due to citation failures and overclaims. Leans **strong reject**."," ","-### Consensus","+### Final Consensus"," "," The gap between R1 and R2 reflects the split between \"interesting idea, poor execution\" vs. \"not ready for any venue.\" The fabrication/placeholder issues tip the balance toward the latter. **Reject** (closer to strong reject)."," "]},{"oldStart":156,"oldLines":10,"newStart":175,"newLines":10,"lines":[" "," This paper is **consistent with a worst-tier dream outcome**. The worst tier is expected to exhibit:"," ","-- **Core negative result with thin support**: The central finding (structure-grounded negatives don't help) is plausible but the evidence is undermined by integrity issues. ✓","-- **Overclaimed conclusions relative to evidence**: Abstract claims exceed what the statistics show. ✓","-- **Incomplete manuscript preparation**: Placeholders, phantom citations, missing hyperparameters, potentially fabricated model names. ✓","-- **Narrow scope with broad claims**: One signal type, one model, one language, but general recommendations. ✓","+- **Core negative result with thin support**: The central finding (structure-grounded negatives don't help) is plausible but the evidence is undermined by integrity issues.","+- **Overclaimed conclusions relative to evidence**: Abstract claims exceed what the statistics show.","+- **Incomplete manuscript preparation**: Placeholders, phantom citations, missing hyperparameters, potentially fabricated model names.","+- **Narrow scope with broad claims**: One signal type, one model, one language, but general recommendations."," "," The paper has a kernel of genuinely useful analysis (Z% sparsity, hop-bounding dilemma, conditional embedding-distance harm) that a better execution could salvage — but in its current form, the manuscript integrity issues make it unsuitable for submission."," "]},{"oldStart":168,"oldLines":10,"newStart":187,"newLines":10,"lines":[" ## Actionable Feedback for Revision"," "," 1. **Fix all placeholder/phantom references immediately.** Every work discussed in text must have a complete reference entry.","-2. **Verify and correct the agent model name** (Qwen3.6-27B → actual model).","-3. **Report all hyperparameters** in a dedicated table or appendix: LoRA rank/alpha, LR, batch size, epochs, τ, InfoNCE temperature, embedding model.","-4. **Soften SWE-bench claims** to match the ±2.5pp CI reality. Use \"consistent trend\" rather than \"underperform across all settings.\"","+2. **Verify and correct the agent model name** (Qwen3.6-27B to actual model).","+3. **Report all hyperparameters** in a dedicated table or appendix: LoRA rank/alpha, LR, batch size, epochs, tau, InfoNCE temperature, embedding model.","+4. **Soften SWE-bench claims** to match the plus/minus 2.5pp CI reality. Use \"consistent trend\" rather than \"underperform across all settings.\""," 5. **Expand related work** to at least 25-30 references covering hard negative mining (ANCE, curriculum HN), code structure (GraphCodeBERT, ASTs), and the missing cited works.","-6. **Ablate τ sensitivity** — even 3 values would strengthen the paper significantly.","+6. **Ablate tau sensitivity** — even 3 values would strengthen the paper significantly."," 7. **Increase manual analysis** to 100+ cases with a second annotator and inter-rater agreement."," 8. **Reframe Eq. 1** to explicitly note the fallback behavior and the 86% degeneracy rate."]}],"originalFile":"# Dream Review Report — worst tier\n\n- **Dream ID**: 20260512T204322\n- **Tier**: worst\n- **Paper**: \"Why Structure-Grounded Hard Negatives Don't Help Code Retrieval (Yet): An Empirical Analysis of Three Negative Mining Strategies\"\n- **Review Mode**: full (2 Reviewers + roundtable)\n- **Overall Score**: reject\n\n---\n\n## Headline Assessment\n\n**Reject.** The paper presents a potentially interesting negative result — structure-grounded hard negatives via CodeQL 2-hop call-graph analysis don't improve code retrieval — but the manuscript has critical integrity issues (fabricated model name, placeholder reference, missing citations for discussed works) and overclaims its statistical evidence. The experimental analysis, while well-structured at a high level, is unreproducible due to missing hyperparameters and has a dangerously thin related work section (9 references). The core empirical insight (Z% sparsity and hop-bounding dilemma) is genuinely informative but buried under execution problems.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1: Methods + Experiments\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Well-structured comparison**: 3 strategies x 3 seeds x 4 evaluation settings is a reasonable experimental matrix for a negative-result paper.\n2. **Z% diagnostic metric**: Defining and measuring the fraction of instances with non-trivial structural signal is a useful methodological contribution. Table 1's breakdown by repository size is informative.\n3. **Hop-bounding tradeoff (Table 4)**: The analysis showing no hop setting simultaneously achieves Z > 20% and timeout < 10% is the paper's strongest analytical result.\n4. **Stratified embedding-distance analysis (Table 6)**: The finding that embedding-distance harm concentrates in high-similarity codebases (p = 0.008) is a salvageable practical insight.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n#### Critical\n\n1. **\"Qwen3.6-27B\" is not a real model.** Qwen models use the Qwen2.5-XB naming convention. No model called \"Qwen3.6-27B\" exists in any public release. This makes the entire SWE-bench Lite evaluation (Table 3) unverifiable. If the agent model is fabricated, the end-to-end results are meaningless.\n\n2. **SweRank reference is a placeholder.** The citation reads \"arXiv:2405.xxxxx\" — a literal unfilled template. This is a basic manuscript integrity failure and would be grounds for desk rejection at any venue.\n\n3. **Missing critical hyperparameters.** The paper does not report: LoRA rank/alpha, learning rate, batch size, number of epochs, warmup schedule, the embedding model used for the embedding-distance strategy, or the InfoNCE temperature. Reproduction is impossible.\n\n4. **Threshold τ is undefined.** The dataflow-aware strategy (§3.2) uses s(x,q) > τ to filter candidates, but τ is never specified, and no sensitivity analysis is provided. This is a free parameter that could significantly affect whether structure-grounded negatives help or harm.\n\n5. **SWE-bench claims overclaim statistical evidence.** The paper acknowledges in §4.3 that \"all three strategies and the SweRank baseline fall within the 95% confidence interval (±2.5pp), making them statistically indistinguishable,\" yet the abstract claims both hard negative strategies underperform random \"across all evaluation settings.\" This is a direct contradiction.\n\n#### Major\n\n6. **Only 3 seeds for variance estimation.** With n=3, the standard deviation estimates (Table 2) have very high uncertainty. The reported ±values are unreliable for distinguishing methods.\n\n7. **Pool size = 200 is unrealistically small.** Real-world code retrieval operates over hundreds to thousands of files per repository. A 200-file pool may not reflect actual retrieval difficulty and inflates absolute recall numbers.\n\n8. **SWE-bench agent confound is uncontrolled.** The LLM agent's ability to use retrieved context may vary across retrieval strategies in ways the evaluation can't isolate. No ablation on agent sensitivity to retrieval quality is provided.\n\n9. **SweRank comparison is structurally unfair.** SweRank is trained on a larger corpus, making it an incomparable baseline. Including it in Table 3 without this caveat in the table caption is misleading.\n\n10. **Table 5 scaling ablation is one-sided.** Only the dataflow strategy is scaled against random. Embedding-distance scaling behavior is missing, preventing a complete picture.\n\n11. **Instance count discrepancy.** Table 1 reports 18,200 instances total, but §3.3 says \"~15K training instances.\" ~3,200 instances were dropped without explanation.\n\n12. **Fusion head ablation (Table 7) is undescribed.** The fusion architecture (concatenation? attention? gating?) is not specified. Results are uninterpretable.\n\n13. **Contribution 2 contains a placeholder.** The text reads \"(Z%)\" where presumably \"14%\" was intended — evidence of incomplete manuscript preparation.\n\n#### Minor\n\n14. **Manual analysis (Table 8)** uses 50 cases with apparently a single annotator. No inter-annotator agreement is reported. Sample size is marginal for a claimed contribution.\n\n15. **InfoNCE loss details** (number of negatives per positive, temperature) are unspecified.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. What is the value of τ, and how sensitive are the dataflow-aware results to this threshold?\n2. What LoRA configuration was used (rank, alpha, target modules)?\n3. What is \"Qwen3.6-27B\"? Is this a typo for an existing model (e.g., Qwen2.5-32B)?\n4. How were the ~15K training instances selected from the 18.2K extracted?\n5. What fusion architecture underlies Table 7?\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2: Novelty + Related Work\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Negative results have value.** The framing as \"empirical analysis\" rather than \"failed experiment\" is appropriate when well-executed.\n2. **Z% sparsity insight.** The discovery that structural signal density drops from 40.8% (pilot) to 14% (full scale) due to medium-repository sampling bias is a genuinely useful methodological warning.\n3. **Conditional embedding-distance finding (§5.3)** is the most actionable result: avoid embedding-distance negatives in high-similarity codebases.\n4. **Failure taxonomy categories are intuitive.** Config/setup and doc-adjacent categories clarifying call-graph blind spots are useful for future work.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n#### Critical\n\n1. **Extremely thin references (9 total).** An empirical analysis comparing negative mining strategies cites only ONE paper on hard negative mining (Robinson et al. 2021). Glaring omissions include:\n   - ANCE (Xiong et al. 2021) — seminal work on approximate nearest neighbor negatives\n   - Kalantidis et al. 2020 — hard negatives in contrastive vision\n   - Curriculum learning / progressive hard negative mining literature\n   - GraphCodeBERT, CodeBERT, UniXcoder — structural code representation models\n   - AST, PDG, control flow graph approaches to code structure\n\n2. **Multiple cited works have NO reference entries.** CoCoHaNeRe, LocAgent, RANGER, and CodexGraph are all discussed in §2 as related work but have no corresponding reference entries. This is a basic scholarly integrity failure — four \"phantom citations.\"\n\n3. **Placeholder reference.** SweRank is cited as \"arXiv:2405.xxxxx\" — an unresolved placeholder. Combined with the phantom citations, this suggests the manuscript was not ready for any form of review.\n\n4. **Abstract overclaims.** \"across all evaluation settings\" is contradicted by the paper's own §4.3 admitting SWE-bench results are \"statistically indistinguishable.\"\n\n#### Major\n\n5. **Narrow scope, broad claims.** Only one structural signal (CodeQL 2-hop call graph), one model (Qwen2.5-Coder-3B), one language (Python). The conclusions and practical recommendations are stated far more broadly than the evidence supports. §7's cross-domain generalization paragraph (molecular networks, circuits, knowledge graphs) is pure speculation.\n\n6. **Eq. 1 formalization is misleading.** The formal definition creates an impression of a principled method, but §3.2 reveals that 86% of the time the method degenerates to random sampling. The gap between the elegant formalization and the degenerate reality is not adequately flagged upfront.\n\n7. **\"Yet\" in title is aspirational, not empirical.** §7 mentions import graphs, type hierarchies, test-source mappings, and learned structural embeddings as future directions but provides zero evidence or pilot data. The \"yet\" suggests forthcoming solutions that the paper does not support.\n\n8. **\"Sufficiently diverse\" qualifier is undefined.** Contribution 1 claims hard negatives underperform \"when the candidate pool is sufficiently diverse.\" This qualifier is never formally defined or experimentally tested. What pool size threshold makes a pool \"sufficiently diverse\"?\n\n9. **Scaling claim overreach.** §5.2 claims \"This rules out the hypothesis that more data would rescue the approach\" based on scaling only to 5K instances, when the full training set is 15K. Testing 1/3 of the data range does not definitively rule out the hypothesis.\n\n#### Minor\n\n10. **Failure taxonomy (50 cases) is undersized** for a standalone \"contribution.\" Single annotator, no inter-rater reliability.\n\n11. **\"Qwen3.6-27B\"** — same fabrication concern flagged by R1. Undermines the entire end-to-end evaluation.\n\n### Story Consistency Analysis\n\n| Check | Verdict |\n|-------|---------|\n| Abstract claims ↔ Results | **Inconsistent** — SWE-bench \"across all settings\" claim contradicts §4.3 CI analysis |\n| Eq. 1 formalization ↔ Actual method | **Misleading** — 86% fallback to random not flagged in formal presentation |\n| Contributions claimed ↔ Delivered | **Partial** — Contribution 1 supported; Contribution 2 has placeholder; Contribution 3 undersized |\n| Title \"Yet\" ↔ Evidence | **Unsupported** — no pilot data for alternative structural signals |\n| Pilot Z=40.8% ↔ Full Z=14% | **Consistent** — adequately explained by sampling bias |\n\n---\n\n## Cross-Reviewer Synthesis (Roundtable Steps 2-3)\n\n### Convergence\n\nBoth reviewers independently converge on **reject** with overlapping critical concerns:\n\n1. **Manuscript integrity failures** — placeholder reference (SweRank arXiv:2405.xxxxx), fabricated model name (Qwen3.6-27B), four phantom citations (CoCoHaNeRe, LocAgent, RANGER, CodexGraph), placeholder in contribution text \"(Z%)\". These alone warrant rejection before evaluating scientific merit.\n\n2. **Overclaimed abstract** — The \"across all evaluation settings\" claim is directly contradicted by the paper's own statistical analysis of SWE-bench results.\n\n3. **Unreproducible** — Missing τ threshold, LoRA config, learning rate, batch size, epochs, embedding model, fusion architecture, InfoNCE temperature.\n\n4. **Inadequate scholarship** — 9 references for an empirical analysis paper; the hard negative mining literature is represented by a single citation.\n\n### Where Reviewers Diverge\n\n- R1 (methods/experiments) finds the experimental *structure* reasonable and gives credit for the hop-bounding analysis and stratified embedding findings. Leans **weak reject**.\n- R2 (novelty/related work) views the manuscript as fundamentally unready due to citation failures and overclaims. Leans **strong reject**.\n\n### Consensus\n\nThe gap between R1 and R2 reflects the split between \"interesting idea, poor execution\" vs. \"not ready for any venue.\" The fabrication/placeholder issues tip the balance toward the latter. **Reject** (closer to strong reject).\n\n---\n\n## Worst-Tier Consistency Assessment\n\nThis paper is **consistent with a worst-tier dream outcome**. The worst tier is expected to exhibit:\n\n- **Core negative result with thin support**: The central finding (structure-grounded negatives don't help) is plausible but the evidence is undermined by integrity issues. ✓\n- **Overclaimed conclusions relative to evidence**: Abstract claims exceed what the statistics show. ✓\n- **Incomplete manuscript preparation**: Placeholders, phantom citations, missing hyperparameters, potentially fabricated model names. ✓\n- **Narrow scope with broad claims**: One signal type, one model, one language, but general recommendations. ✓\n\nThe paper has a kernel of genuinely useful analysis (Z% sparsity, hop-bounding dilemma, conditional embedding-distance harm) that a better execution could salvage — but in its current form, the manuscript integrity issues make it unsuitable for submission.\n\n---\n\n## Actionable Feedback for Revision\n\n1. **Fix all placeholder/phantom references immediately.** Every work discussed in text must have a complete reference entry.\n2. **Verify and correct the agent model name** (Qwen3.6-27B → actual model).\n3. **Report all hyperparameters** in a dedicated table or appendix: LoRA rank/alpha, LR, batch size, epochs, τ, InfoNCE temperature, embedding model.\n4. **Soften SWE-bench claims** to match the ±2.5pp CI reality. Use \"consistent trend\" rather than \"underperform across all settings.\"\n5. **Expand related work** to at least 25-30 references covering hard negative mining (ANCE, curriculum HN), code structure (GraphCodeBERT, ASTs), and the missing cited works.\n6. **Ablate τ sensitivity** — even 3 values would strengthen the paper significantly.\n7. **Increase manual analysis** to 100+ cases with a second annotator and inter-rater agreement.\n8. **Reframe Eq. 1** to explicitly note the fallback behavior and the 86% degeneracy rate.\n","userModified":false}}
